 名/票券: https://forms.gle/HFYpFXiXH7NtERR18\n\nLunch will be served 
 — RSVP Requested.\n\nArt Historian Dr. Kirsten Pai Buick\, author of Chi
 ld of the Fire—the first major study of the famed​​ Afro-Native neo-
 classical sculptor Edmonia Lewis (1844-1907)—discusses the artist with T
 APS Professor Jennifer DeVere Brody\, whose recent book Moving Stones also
  centers the work of this remarkable figure whose international profession
 al career served as a model.\n\nThis event is sponsored by Stanford TAPS\,
  the Department of African & African American Studies\, the Department of 
 Art and Art History\, and CCSRE's Imagining Justice.\n\nABOUT JENNIFER DEV
 ERE BRODY'S BOOK MOVING STONES | Moving Stones explores the extraordinary 
 life and work of Edmonia Lewis\, the Black and Ojibwe sculptor who rose to
  international fame in the nineteenth century. Blending biography\, histor
 y\, and theory\, Jennifer DeVere Brody approaches Lewis’s legacy through
  a Black feminist and queer lens\, illuminating how her sculptures and sel
 f-fashioning challenged constraints of her time. Living much of her life i
 n Rome as a free Afro-Native woman\, Lewis used neoclassical forms to carv
 e out a life in art. Brody considers how Lewis’s works were viewed histo
 rically and how they resonate with postmodern artists\, engaging themes of
  race\, materiality\, sexuality\, and embodiment. Rethinking one of the mo
 st important sculptors of her era\, Moving Stones shows how Lewis’s art 
 continues to inspire contemporary artists and scholars today.\n\nABOUT KIR
